===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|head=[[Moore]][[-'s|'s]] [[law]]}}

# {{lb|en|computing}} An [[empirical]] [[observation]] that the [[density]] of [[transistor]]s on a [[chip]] [[double]]s every two [[year]]s. <!--often misquoted as every 18 months-->
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-03-04|author={{w|Alice Rawsthorn}}|title=Farewell, Pocket Calculator?|journal={{w|The  New York Times}}|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/03/05/arts/design/farewell-pocket-calculator.html|passage=The pocket calculator’s fortunes have fallen ever since. It is a victim of “'''Moore’s Law''',” the theory that the number of transistors that can be squeezed on to a microchip will roughly double every two years, thereby increasing computing power at the same rate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Arnold Thackray|author2=David C. Brock|author3=Rachel Jones|title=Moore's Law: The Life of Gordon Moore, Silicon Valley's Quiet Revolutionary|publisher=Hachette UK|isbn=9780465055623|url=https://books.google.de/books?id=TuQ4DgAAQBAJ&lpg=PT400&dq=%22Moore's%20law%22&pg=PT400#v=onepage&q=%22Moore's%20law%22&f=false
|passage=How will industries and enterprises dependent upon and accustomed to '''Moore's Law''' continue to thrive? What might this disruption mean for society? And will the certainty of continued exponential change enshrined in '''Moore's Law''' ever become standard again?}}

